{
  "dom_id": "step-q77214",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$",
  "worked_solution": "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
